{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Re-scoped to a regression-guard ticket (original premise inverted by merged change 2345)\n\nClaimed and worked under /rebar-debug. **The original defect is obsolete.** The ticket was filed asserting the graceful-drain contract is broken because sync certified tool bodies (`review_plan`/`verify_completion`/`review_code`/`scan_spec`) run ON the event-loop thread. As of MERGED change **2345** (`25ea12c200ae`, ticket d240 dewy-rotatable-tarsier), that premise is INVERTED and the assumption is now TRUE.\n\n### Confirmed on current origin/main (worktree at 91cf9b46, = origin/main; 2345 is an ancestor of HEAD)\n- `offload_sync_tools` (`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py`) rewrites every sync tool body into an async wrapper that runs it on an `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` worker and marks the tool `is_async`, so the SDK `await`s it and the event loop stays free. `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` (not `asyncio.to_thread`) is used because it copies the caller's contextvars into the worker — the op-cert signer binding depends on it.\n- `wire_health` applies `instrument_certified_tools` FIRST, then `offload_sync_tools` SECOND (load-bearing order, documented in-code).\n- The drain is BOUNDED: `drain_then_exit` computes `deadline = monotonic() + grace_seconds`; `make_sigterm_handler` stops intake (`should_exit`) then drains within that bound.\n\nRather than a live SIGTERM/blue-green experiment (impractical locally), I settled AC#1 with the focused unit/integration coverage that actually pins the property (per the skill's guidance).\n\n### What I pinned (regression guards)\nThe off-loop property and bounded drain were **already** pinned by existing tests, which I confirmed pass on current main:\n- Off-loop execution: `tests/unit/test_mcp_tool_offload.py::test_a_sync_tool_body_runs_off_the_event_loop_thread`, `::test_a_slow_sync_tool_does_not_block_concurrent_work`, `::test_contextvars_bound_by_the_caller_reach_the_offloaded_body`; certified-tool off-loop via the gauge in `::test_certified_tools_keep_their_gauge_instrumentation_after_the_offload` and `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_wired_server_moves_gauge_for_a_really_registered_certified_tool`.\n- Bounded drain (both cases): `test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_drain_then_exit_waits_for_gauge_then_exits_within_bound` and `::test_drain_then_exit_is_bounded_when_gauge_never_drains`; handler ordering in `::test_make_sigterm_handler_stops_intake_then_drains`.\n\n### AC#3 — the genuine gap: fail-loud guard (RED→GREEN)\n`instrument_certified_tools` previously **silently skipped** a certified tool that was already `is_async` at instrument time. Given the load-bearing FIRST-instrument-then-offload order, a certified tool must NOT be async there; if it were (a `wire_health` reorder, or a certified tool natively converted to `async def`), the sync gauge wrapper becomes a no-op on it, the in-flight gauge stops counting billable work, and the SIGTERM drain fails OPEN with no signal.\n- New RED test `test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_instrument_certified_tools_fails_loud_on_an_already_async_certified_tool` — verified RED first (\"DID NOT RAISE RuntimeError\") against unmodified main, GREEN after the guard; mutation check (revert guard → silent skip) returns it to RED (run under an OS sandbox).\n- Fix: `instrument_certified_tools` now raises `RuntimeError` naming the offending certified tool when it is `is_async` at instrument time (the `tool is None` skip for unregistered builds is unchanged). This is **non-behavioral in the correct (production) order** — the guard never fires there; it only converts a currently-impossible misconfiguration from silent-fail-open into a loud test/startup failure.\n- Updated the existing `::test_certified_tool_instrumentation_moves_the_gauge`, which had asserted the old silent-skip contract (`async certified tool left untouched`), to drop that now-outlawed assertion (moved to the fail-loud test) while keeping its gauge-movement purpose. This is a deliberate contract change, not test-weakening.\n\n### AC#2 — docs\nCorrected `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md` (Decision 2 + the \"never kills an in-flight certified op\" consequence) and `docs/adr/0079-autodeploy-on-box-timer.md` (retire-path SIGTERM drain) to state the REAL threading model: a sync MCP tool body is offloaded to an anyio worker via `offload_sync_tools`, and the drain contract holds because of that off-loop execution PLUS the bounded grace window — not because the SDK offloads on its own.\n\n`file_impact` set. Gates (`make lint`, `make typecheck`) green; full `make test` in progress. Landing via Gerrit; will close after `Verified +1`.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788075372002949669,"uuid":"5cd5e7dd-0f95-4cbb-9c32-05dd4814fbd8"}